177
00:27:49.840 --> 00:28:04.189
Richard Bair: For the members of the TSC who want to dig into this, I would recommend going and reading the specification in detail. And again, like I said, it's really good for feeding to an LLM, so you can give it to your AI and ask it all kinds of questions to help sort of learn it.

178
00:28:04.300 --> 00:28:21.549
Richard Bair: And of course, you can also, plow through and read it. It was meant to be human-readable. So there are a few, important concepts, and because there's always these two different ledgers that are involved in Clipper, there's… there's…

179
00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:35.930
Richard Bair: we've given them names, and we've given it a name based on the flow direction of sort of an originating message. Fundamentally, what you're trying to do is send some type of message from one ledger to another ledger.

180
00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:48.619
Richard Bair: When we are using a terminology of the other ledger without a sense of direction, we just call it the peer ledger. It's like, that's… the peer is the other ledger that you're talking to.

181
00:28:49.840 --> 00:29:06.550
Richard Bair: Now, the whole system is predicated on state proofs, and if you're not deeply familiar with the state proof, essentially what it does is, on any particular ledger, you have state, and state is some…

182
00:29:06.670 --> 00:29:20.210
Richard Bair: data that is stored on the ledger itself. It's data stored on the ledger. And what a state proof does is it provides a cryptographic proof that that data was in the ledger.

183
00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:27.020
Richard Bair: as of some block, some point in time. You have a mathematical proof that that data was in the ledger.

184
00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:29.640
Richard Bair: Now…

185
00:29:29.710 --> 00:29:46.630
Richard Bair: an endpoint in our terminology, in this specification, an endpoint is some node or server that's responsible for periodically communicating with the endpoints in the peer ledger. So the…

186
00:29:46.700 --> 00:30:03.659
Richard Bair: one of the design centers of this system was, we want… we actually want the ledgers talking to each other. It's kind of like a… it's not a gossip protocol in the normal gossip sense, but it's like a communication protocol that

187
00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:26.980
Richard Bair: two ledgers can both implement, and then they can just talk directly to each other. Just like within any one ledger, you can implement a gossip protocol, and all the nodes in that ledger talk to each other. In this case, the endpoint protocol is something that nodes and two ledgers can implement, and then they can talk to each other. So you truly get this idea that it's ledger talking to ledger, and there's no bridge entity

188
00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:31.000
Richard Bair: that has to be between these two things. Now.

189
00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:34.619
Richard Bair: As a matter of practicality.

190
00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:59.539
Richard Bair: The endpoint communication system is optional. Like, you don't have to have it. Fundamentally, you could… anybody in the world could just go get a state proof from Ledger A, and give it to Ledger B, and the system works. But the endpoint protocol makes it so that it happens seamlessly behind the scenes, and you never have to think about it, or deal with it, or do anything.

191
00:30:59.540 --> 00:31:03.240
Richard Bair: It's like, it allows the ledgers to talk to each other directly.

192
00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:18.520
Richard Bair: Now, I mentioned the Clipper service, and this is really the core business logic in all of that on-chain data, all that state that implements Clipper on a particular ledger. For Hiero.

193
00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:42.420
Richard Bair: the Clipper service is a… is a service, just like our other services, meaning it's written in the ledger's, you know, Java code, it's running as a native service, if you will, on the ledger. On other ledgers, like, you know, Ethereum or any EVM ledger, you can deploy the core business logic, the Clipper service, as a smart contract, as a Solidity smart contract.

194
00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:48.250
Richard Bair: And that's one of the repos that we also opened up yesterday, is,

195
00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:59.769
Richard Bair: has the smart contract implementations of a Clipper service. So if you were to come in here, you'd be able to find the Clipper service.

196
00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:10.020
Richard Bair: Here, and you can see how that would be implemented. And, you know, we hope someday there will be an implementation for, all different ledgers.

197
00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:25.990
Richard Bair: You know, at least in the interim, ledgers will be implementing Clipper services, or we or somebody in the community can implement the Clipper service as, you know, in the smart contract language that that language… that that ledger supports.

198
00:32:25.990 --> 00:32:32.470
Richard Bair: So, for example, we'd like to see a Rust implementation on… Solana.

199
00:32:32.580 --> 00:32:34.199
Richard Bair: In…

200
00:32:34.330 --> 00:32:47.489
Richard Bair: In the future, hopefully, you know, as Clipper becomes more widespread, Ledgers will actually start building it in native the same way that we did on Hiero, because that gives you the most efficient way of being able to do it.

201
00:32:48.250 --> 00:32:55.280
Richard Bair: But anyway, so that's the Clipper service. It has all of the core logic, and we'll see what that is a little bit later.

202
00:32:55.960 --> 00:33:15.400
Richard Bair: A channel is basically an on-ledger entity. It's something that's stored in state of this clipper service, but it represents the communication channel between two peer ledgers. So, and you can have multiple channels between two different ledgers.

203
00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:33.180
Richard Bair: I expect that mostly there will be a very small number, somewhere between, you know, 1 and 3, or 1 in 5 of these things, because there's not a lot of advantage to operating additional channels, but there is a reason why we support multiple channels, which it can…

204
00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:43.189
Richard Bair: find out more in the spec if we don't get to it. But, but basically, channel just represents a connection between two different ledgers, sort of a logical connection.

205
00:33:43.430 --> 00:33:56.839
Richard Bair: Then we have this thing called a connector. Now, the connector is interesting, in that it solves one of the fundamental problems in inter-ledger communication, and it solves it in a way that's quite unique.

206
00:33:56.900 --> 00:34:13.990
Richard Bair: relative to other inter-ledger communication systems. This connector is an economic entity. It's not a… it's an entity that pays for the transit of messages from one ledger to another.

207
00:34:13.989 --> 00:34:24.810
Richard Bair: Fundamentally, if you're sending a message from Ledger A to Ledger B, somebody on Ledger B has to front the cost of the gas.

208
00:34:24.810 --> 00:34:34.040
Richard Bair: to deliver that message. And the question is, who is going to front it? And that's actually a very important question from a security perspective, because

209
00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:58.930
Richard Bair: In some interledger communication protocols, it's the equivalent of the endpoint. It's the thing that… it's some server that was watching the blockchain on Ledger A, and then creates a transaction and submits it to Ledger B. They front the gas, and then they get reimbursed if, you know, the thing executes right. But what happens if they fronted the gas and they said.

210
00:34:58.930 --> 00:35:06.459
Richard Bair: They submitted a transaction, and it ended up failing in some way that left the endpoint holding the bag.

211
00:35:06.460 --> 00:35:16.189
Richard Bair: That's a little bit problematic, because it means that an attacker on ledger A could basically drain the funds of the endpoint. So, using this connector system.

212
00:35:16.390 --> 00:35:29.510
Richard Bair: which you'll see down below, it solves for this. It makes it so that the entity that ends up fronting the cost of the gas agreed to do so by its

213
00:35:29.510 --> 00:35:43.700
Richard Bair: here, there's basically a connector on each ledger. When you send a message from ledger A, the connector, your connect… the connector… and there can be many connectors, by the way, there can be many connectors. So when I think of a connector, I think of it as, like.

214
00:35:43.750 --> 00:36:05.950
Richard Bair: DHL, FedEx, UPS, etc, right? There can be multiple of these entities, but what is their job? Their job is to pay for moving your message from A to B, and so you'll have, like, let's say you have a Foo connector on ledger A, and you have a Foo connector on ledger B. That connector is owned by the same entity.

215
00:36:05.950 --> 00:36:14.619
Richard Bair: And on Ledger A, when you send your message, that connector will agree or not to send your message. If it agrees to send your message.

216
00:36:14.620 --> 00:36:21.549
Richard Bair: then the half of that food connector that's on Ledger B is obligated to pay for it. So essentially.

217
00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:46.050
Richard Bair: Connectors make it so that it's not two different actors, one on one ledger, who's obligating somebody on the second ledger to pay. It's the same actor on both ledgers who's obligating itself to pay, and that eliminates the entire attack vector, where some random actor on one ledger causes a different actor on the second ledger to have to pay.

218
00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:46.800
Richard Bair: pay.

219
00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:54.339
Richard Bair: It eliminates that by saying, no, it's the same actor on both ledgers who creates that obligation. So this is a connector.

220
00:36:55.510 --> 00:37:19.800
Richard Bair: Messages are the actual bytes that we're sending from one place to another. A bundle is an ordered batch of messages that get communicated. And then there's some specific types of messages. There's a data message, which is your normal application message, you know, set of bytes. It's basically, as far as Clipper's concerned, it's just bytes. And it's the application that determines what those bytes are that are going to get sent.

221
00:37:19.970 --> 00:37:33.519
Richard Bair: A response message is part of the protocol, it's a special type of message, so that when you send something from a source ledger to the destination ledger, then it has to respond and tell you what happened to that message.

222
00:37:33.750 --> 00:37:40.670
Richard Bair: So this is kind of like an HTTP response message, in some sense. And, that's part of the protocol.

223
00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:51.959
Richard Bair: And then you have control messages, which are, again, protocol messages where we are sending some protocol-specific data between the ledgers.

224
00:37:52.930 --> 00:37:59.690
Richard Bair: Now, we call these ledgers a source ledger and destination ledger most of the time. When we don't have a…

225
00:37:59.690 --> 00:38:24.669
Richard Bair: directionality in our terminology, we'll call it a peer ledger, but when there is… there often will be directionality in the way that we describe things. You have a source ledger and a destination ledger. The source ledger is where the message originated from, destination ledger is where the message was heading, response messages go back from destination back to the source, so there's, like, this real sense of directionality that we'll use these terms

226
00:38:24.670 --> 00:38:26.610
Richard Bair: Source and destination ledger.

227
00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:39.539
Richard Bair: There's configuration, of course, to indicate various different things, and then, HashSphere, we just talked about, as a primary permission network, writing Hiero software, but it could be…

228
00:38:39.610 --> 00:38:52.940
Richard Bair: Honestly, this… probably, we need to update the spec, because the spec is… is ledger agnostic. We may use HashSphere in here as sort of an example, but it's certainly not limited to it. Any ledger in the world would be able to implement this.

229
00:38:52.940 --> 00:39:00.509
Richard Bair: As long as they have the ability to generate some kind of cryptographic state proof. That's ultimately what we need.

230
00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:10.359
Richard Bair: Now, at a high level, this diagram, I think, is fairly helpful. See if we can make it bigger.

231
00:39:11.230 --> 00:39:20.140
Richard Bair: Okay, for kind of understanding overall what the flow of messages look like from a source ledger to a destination ledger.

232
00:39:20.490 --> 00:39:27.099
Richard Bair: So, the way that it works is that you start off with an application, and the application is any smart contract that you want to write.

233
00:39:27.110 --> 00:39:41.840
Richard Bair: Right? You write your application. And in your application, you're going to use APIs of the Clipper service in order to send a message to an application on the destination ledger. It could be your own application on both sides, I think that's

234
00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:47.609
Richard Bair: pretty common. But if another application on the destination ledger has some kind of a

235
00:39:47.640 --> 00:39:52.400
Richard Bair: API that you can invoke from the source, then they could be two different applications.

236
00:39:52.500 --> 00:40:06.040
Richard Bair: So what we'll do is we're gonna make… we're gonna… we're gonna make a call down to the Clipper service and say, here is some message that I want to send to this application on this destination ledger.

237
00:40:06.150 --> 00:40:10.040
Richard Bair: And this is the channel that I want to use.

238
00:40:10.370 --> 00:40:16.459
Richard Bair: And this is the connector that I'm going to use. So the application specifies all of those things.

239
00:40:16.460 --> 00:40:33.420
Richard Bair: It specifies the channel to use to communicate with the destination ledger, it specifies the connector, who's going to pay for the transmission of this message, and it specifies the destination application, or the application on the destination ledger that's going to be invoked. And of course, it supplies the bytes.

240
00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:48.320
Richard Bair: When it does that, the first thing the Clipper service does is it goes to the connector. You said this connector is going to pay for the transmission? Let me go ask the connector. Are you willing to pay for this transmission? And the connector can say yes or no.

241
00:40:48.610 --> 00:40:51.060
Richard Bair: The connector could also require payment.

242
00:40:51.060 --> 00:41:09.370
Richard Bair: So, when you send your application, it could be that the connector is owned by you, and you're like, don't worry about payment, just say, yes, I'm gonna send the bytes. Or the connector could be operated… owned by a third party, in which case, maybe they want to say, sure, I'm willing to send whatever bytes you want to send, but it's going to cost you, and they charge you a certain amount.

243
00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:28.359
Richard Bair: It's up to them how they want to write that. It's just a normal contract that you deploy. It's totally a open, decentralized system in that anybody can deploy a connector contract, and then anybody who sends an application can choose to use that connector contract from the cooker service.

244
00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:39.889
Richard Bair: Okay, so you've created your message, you've sent it to the Clipper service, the Clipper service checked with the connector, the connector said, sure, I will authorize the sending of this message.

245
00:41:39.890 --> 00:41:48.500
Richard Bair: The Clipper service then puts your message into a queue within the channel that you… that you selected.

246
00:41:49.530 --> 00:41:52.510
Richard Bair: Messages in the channel are ordered.

247
00:41:52.740 --> 00:41:55.220
Richard Bair: And that's a very important thing.

248
00:41:55.330 --> 00:42:03.560
Richard Bair: Because what it's going to allow us to do is to very efficiently send a big chunk of messages across.

249
00:42:03.770 --> 00:42:20.580
Richard Bair: and verify them with a single proof. It's also going to give you that guarantee order of delivery is also important for simplifying your application. If you send a message, and then you send a second message, you know that they will be received in order on the opposite side.

250
00:42:20.800 --> 00:42:40.489
Richard Bair: And this is also really important for making sure that you have fairness. There's no… there's no way that these messages are going to be reordered by any entity anywhere in the system, which means no front-running. No… no malicious user being able to reconfigure the order in which these things happened.

251
00:42:40.490 --> 00:42:57.209
Richard Bair: Whatever the consensus order of those messages are on your source ledger, that will be the order that they get handled on the destination ledger. So, that's a very important attribute. So, each one of the messages that we want to send, they get put into this outbound queue.

252
00:42:57.210 --> 00:43:15.660
Richard Bair: Now, as far as the source ledger's consensus time is concerned, that's when it's done. It did its job. It took your message, it verified it with the connector, the connector said it's willing to pay, it stuck it into the channel state, it's…

253
00:43:15.750 --> 00:43:18.260
Richard Bair: Done Now…

254
00:43:18.530 --> 00:43:33.290
Richard Bair: An endpoint, or, like we said before, it doesn't have to be an endpoint, it could be any random user in the world, is going to periodically go grab a state proof of what is in the channel state.

255
00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:41.790
Richard Bair: And then it is going to pass that bundle of messages, the channel state, essentially, with a proof.

256
00:43:42.120 --> 00:44:00.060
Richard Bair: to an endpoint on the other ledger, or it could just call directly into the other ledger. But this diagram is showing the preferred design, which is where you have endpoints involved. So, this endpoint node right here, what it's doing is periodically grabbing this state and a proof.

257
00:44:00.490 --> 00:44:01.930
Richard Bair: Looking up.

258
00:44:02.050 --> 00:44:09.580
Richard Bair: an endpoint on the pier, dialing it up using gRPC, and saying, here is a bundle in proof.

259
00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:27.409
Richard Bair: The destination endpoint will verify that proof and say, yep, this is a valid bundle, and by the way, it can check its own understanding of state on its destination side and say… and it contains some messages that we haven't seen before, so we're going to deliver this bundle.

260
00:44:28.110 --> 00:44:33.009
Richard Bair: It delivers the bundle to its own ledger for consensus.

261
00:44:33.660 --> 00:44:43.240
Richard Bair: Now, once it reaches consensus, in our case on Hiero, it means that once you've gossiped the transaction and we get to handle

262
00:44:43.240 --> 00:44:59.620
Richard Bair: on Ethereum, it would mean once your transac- you know, once this transaction got included in a block, whatever is appropriate for your particular ledger, once it reaches consensus, then what the Clipper service is going to do is it's going to take that bundle.

263
00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:23.739
Richard Bair: verify the proof itself, of course, because it can't trust its own endpoints, you never know what somebody's going to give you, so it re-verifies the bundle, checks to see if there's any work to do. And if there is, it handles each one of those messages in order. And for each message, it's going to first go charge the connector on the destination side. It's going to say, alright, connector.

264
00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:42.849
Richard Bair: your peer connector on the other side said you would pay, you need to… you need to pay for the gas that we're going to use. And then it goes to the application, and it delivers the message, and then the application does whatever it wants with it. And then the, and then it generates a response that it can send

265
00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:59.099
Richard Bair: back. And the responses are just normal messages, so even though this shows an outbound queue with R1, R2, R3, in reality it's intermixed response messages, data messages, control messages, sort of intermixed in this queue. And then what happens is that

266
00:45:59.320 --> 00:46:04.809
Richard Bair: Those, those messages go back in the same mechanism that we used to get them here.

267
00:46:04.810 --> 00:46:28.279
Richard Bair: This endpoint periodically grabs all the state and the state proof, dials up its peer endpoint, gives it the data, this guy calls his Clipper service. Once it gets to handling the transaction, it starts processing those messages. So some of the messages that Clipper Service is processing are data messages, as in this, you know, simple flow where it's this message, M1, that comes across, handles it.

268
00:46:28.280 --> 00:46:38.779
Richard Bair: Gets the connector to pay for it, delivers it to the application, puts a response in its queue, and sometimes those messages are response messages that come through, where it looks at it and says,

269
00:46:38.790 --> 00:46:54.310
Richard Bair: I now have a response for message M1, I can evict M1 from state, and I… because I now know what happened with it. And there's a lot of details around what those response messages can do, what types of responses you can get, all that kind of stuff.

270
00:46:55.170 --> 00:47:13.389
Richard Bair: So, I guess the main point from this, at a very high level, is to just say that when you send a message from your application, you call the Clipper service, it verifies with a connector that it is willing to pay all the destination side for this transaction.

271
00:47:13.580 --> 00:47:17.440
Richard Bair: It then puts it into an ordered queue that is in state.

272
00:47:17.710 --> 00:47:41.219
Richard Bair: An endpoint where some actor then periodically grabs that state proof and the current bundle of messages, submits it to the other side, which submits it as a transaction to the Clipper service on the other side, which then handles each message, pays for each message from the connector, which had obligated itself on the source, and then sends response messages back.

273
00:47:41.800 --> 00:47:48.359
Richard Bair: That's the fundamental, flow of communication flow between these ledgers.

274
00:47:50.330 --> 00:47:58.730
Richard Bair: There's a whole section here on the trust model, the trust chain, how that all works, we'll skip that for today.

275
00:47:59.590 --> 00:48:01.669
Richard Bair: I think,

276
00:48:02.500 --> 00:48:13.619
Richard Bair: let's see if there's another crucial concept to get through before you have a chance to go through and read a little bit about it.

277
00:48:19.070 --> 00:48:22.960
Richard Bair: Okay, so this diagram is a good one, we won't go through it right now.

278
00:48:23.580 --> 00:48:31.840
Richard Bair: But it gets into way more detail on exactly how the message queues, how we track

279
00:48:32.020 --> 00:48:47.819
Richard Bair: where you're at, the running hashes, all that kind of information that's in here. It's a good one to really dig into and understand. It sort of forms the basis of it. Alright, let's talk about the proof system here for a second.

280
00:48:47.820 --> 00:48:52.929
Richard Bair: I think this is a good… a good concept to also get across. So…

281
00:48:53.210 --> 00:49:02.540
Richard Bair: One of the questions that people would ask me when I would share with them the Clipper design under, you know, the last few months, is that they would say, well.

282
00:49:02.540 --> 00:49:17.559
Richard Bair: But how does it work? Like, you know, in all the existing systems, there was some kind of… I guess maybe not all of them, I think there's a few that are a little bit closer to what we're doing, but if you were to look at the bridges.

283
00:49:17.560 --> 00:49:42.469
Richard Bair: layer zeros and chain links and CCTPs and so forth of the world. They all base… they're based on an attestation model. There's some third party who watches the blockchain on ledger A, on the source ledger, and then they send… they attest to the destination ledger, saying, I, the trusted third party, saw this on the source ledger, so you can go ahead and handle this on the destination

284
00:49:42.470 --> 00:49:43.070
Richard Bair: Thank you so much.

285
00:49:43.530 --> 00:50:06.680
Richard Bair: And that was a design that we were never comfortable with, because you inherently have this trusted, centralized third party who's doing attestations. And some of the bridges had ways of trying to decentralize that a bit, but it was… fundamentally, you have this third party that you're trusting, and we thought, you know, we really want this to be ledger trusting ledger.

286
00:50:06.890 --> 00:50:11.669
Richard Bair: We went through all this effort as an industry in order to create these, you know.

287
00:50:11.690 --> 00:50:34.459
Richard Bair: incredibly sophisticated consensus systems, so that we had trusted ledgers, and then we put a third party with a trust me bro in the middle, and we just never thought that was very smart. So what we really wanted was the ability to trust one ledger with another, but how do you do that? The consensus systems of all these different ledgers are fundamentally

288
00:50:34.560 --> 00:50:37.950
Richard Bair: very unique, right? They are quite different.

289
00:50:38.440 --> 00:50:53.380
Richard Bair: And so how do you get one ledger to trust another ledger? Well, the answer to this is state proof. Basically, any given ledger that has state has some data that's stored on-chain.

290
00:50:53.880 --> 00:50:55.170
Richard Bair: Gates…

291
00:50:55.360 --> 00:51:07.270
Richard Bair: that data being changed by its consensus system. So, by proxy, any data that ends up in state has passed through their consensus system.

292
00:51:07.270 --> 00:51:22.409
Richard Bair: without even having to know what their consensus system is. We don't… it doesn't even matter, because all you're doing is saying, if I trust that ledger, then any data that is in its state, that I can prove is in its state.

293
00:51:22.780 --> 00:51:33.690
Richard Bair: I know was approved by its consensus system. So that's very powerful. It lets you leverage the consensus system without even knowing what that consensus system is.

294
00:51:34.050 --> 00:51:51.590
Richard Bair: Now, the state proof themselves, there's several different kinds, depending on the particular ledger. There's not just one universal state-proof system in the world. And therein lies the next difficulty. Now that I don't need to know about every

295
00:51:51.740 --> 00:51:58.490
Richard Bair: consensus system in the world, but there's multiple different state-proof systems. How do you do this?

296
00:51:58.630 --> 00:52:04.210
Richard Bair: We could have done it where we just fully specified every state-proof system in the world.

297
00:52:04.210 --> 00:52:27.119
Richard Bair: But that's an ever-changing set, and there will always be some ledger who's on the outside looking in. Some ledger that their state-proof system has not yet been published, and then they have to publish it, and then they have to get a new version of the Clipper service upgraded on all the different networks in order to support the new state-proof system.

298
00:52:27.140 --> 00:52:45.220
Richard Bair: It's a mess. That would be horrible. And when we go post-quantum, and all these things change, we'd have to go roll out updates to everybody in the world. It's never gonna work, right? You're never gonna have, like, a universally updated Clipper service out there in the world. It would be very… it'd just be a horrible challenge.

299
00:52:45.320 --> 00:52:48.990
Richard Bair: And it would be very centralized in terms of,

300
00:52:49.280 --> 00:52:59.619
Richard Bair: very difficult, right? Sort of a lot of friction to get your new ledger with its glorious and beautiful new state-proof system supported by the world. So, what else could we do?

301
00:52:59.980 --> 00:53:20.719
Richard Bair: Well, what we did was we said, let's decouple this further. Let us say that you have a ledger, and it has some state-proof system. And let's say that the destination ledger doesn't even care what your state-proof system is. As long as your state-proof system can be verified by a contract on the destination, you're fine.

302
00:53:20.940 --> 00:53:22.460
Richard Bair: In other words.

303
00:53:22.460 --> 00:53:46.969
Richard Bair: I… if… let's say that I created, well, let's use our example on Hiero. We are creating a state-proof system based on TSS. Nobody else in the world has our state-proof system. And let's say that Clipper was already out in all the world, and everybody was using it to talk between ledgers, and my poor, you know, Hiero ledger isn't able to participate because I have this glorious new state-proof system that's so beautiful and wonderful.

304
00:53:47.690 --> 00:53:51.579
Richard Bair: How do I get anybody to use this thing? Well, what I would do…

305
00:53:51.860 --> 00:54:05.369
Richard Bair: is I have my state proof system on my ledger, great. What I'm gonna do is I'm gonna write a contract, a verifier contract, and this contract is able to verify my glorious TSS state proof.

306
00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:27.790
Richard Bair: Nobody else on any of the other ledgers knows about my verifier contract, or my state-proof system, or anything else. Nobody in Ethereum has to vote on it, nobody in Solana has to care. I just write my contract that's able to verify my state-proof system. I go deploy my contract into the other ledgers, and that contract is now deployed in the ledger, and it can verify my state-proof system.

307
00:54:27.790 --> 00:54:39.170
Richard Bair: Now, I'm going to go establish a new channel between their ledger and my ledger, and on that channel, I'm going to say, and this is the verifier contract.

308
00:54:39.560 --> 00:54:47.409
Richard Bair: Now, any application that wants to send stuff from my ledger to their ledger, or from their ledger to mine.

309
00:54:47.470 --> 00:54:58.370
Richard Bair: well, let's say specifically from my ledger to theirs, anybody who wants to do that, they just say, hey, I'm going to use that connector that I set up, and

310
00:54:58.370 --> 00:55:09.149
Richard Bair: and these messages will go over the connector, and when it lands on the destination ledger, it will use my verifier contract to verify my fancy TSS state proof that was established on my ledger.

311
00:55:09.500 --> 00:55:19.970
Richard Bair: In other words, it's decoupled. I didn't have to coordinate with anybody else in the world. I was able to deploy my… write my own state-proof system.

312
00:55:20.200 --> 00:55:34.829
Richard Bair: I, as the ledger owner, right, wrote my state-proof system, I wrote my verifier contract, I deployed that verifier contract, and I established the channels between my ledger and other ledgers. And now we're able to have information flow.

313
00:55:36.120 --> 00:55:38.880
Richard Bair: Now, of course, it doesn't have to be the…

314
00:55:39.150 --> 00:55:50.610
Richard Bair: the contract… Sorry, the ledger owner who writes the verifier contract, or creates the channel. So let's say that we had a ledger out there, who, you know.

315
00:55:50.630 --> 00:56:07.340
Richard Bair: they weren't going to lift a finger to support Clipper, because they just didn't care, or know about it, or didn't on board, or whatever. If I, as a interested third party, looked at their system and said, oh, look, they have a state-proof system, that's fantastic.

316
00:56:07.340 --> 00:56:23.839
Richard Bair: I can write the verifier contract, I can deploy it to the ledgers I want to connect to their ledger, I can set up a channel, I can deploy the Clipper service to their network, I can set all those things up without having to, you know, go through their foundation or anything else. We can… we can just set it up, make it work.

317
00:56:23.840 --> 00:56:33.649
Richard Bair: Because everything that we've built, this entire design, is essentially… the only thing that it really requires from a ledger

318
00:56:34.010 --> 00:56:45.079
Richard Bair: is that it has a smart contract system, a permissionless smart contract system I can deploy to, and it has a state-proof system. As long as those two things are true.

319
00:56:45.080 --> 00:56:55.170
Richard Bair: I, as a third party, can enable Clipper on that ledger, and it turns out there are a lot of ledgers that have those attributes, that have those properties.

320
00:56:55.170 --> 00:57:05.760
Richard Bair: There's a way to wire up to virtually every ledger that we have looked at, all the major ones, because they have these properties. And,

321
00:57:05.760 --> 00:57:11.909
Richard Bair: So it's really powerful. So I think there's one of the things that is really exciting about Clipper is

322
00:57:11.910 --> 00:57:33.299
Richard Bair: is that it's not one of these situations where you have to go convince the world to use it. It's something where if you are interested in Clipper, you can write and or deploy a Clipper service to a ledger of your choice. You can establish the Clipper verifier to trust that. You can create the channel

323
00:57:33.440 --> 00:57:52.220
Richard Bair: between the ledgers that you're interested in. You can write the application that's going to send messages from one site to another. You can deploy… write and deploy the connector contracts that will pay for sending those messages between ledgers. You literally can do all of those things.

324
00:57:52.220 --> 00:57:56.760
Richard Bair: In a totally decentralized way without any third party having to be involved.

325
00:57:56.760 --> 00:58:00.319
Richard Bair: As long as the ledger supports a state-proof system.

326
00:58:00.840 --> 00:58:18.189
Richard Bair: You can do it. And if you, as a third party, look at a ledger and you say, oh, there is a Clipper service deployed, you can independently verify that that Clipper service is a good implementation of the Clipper service spec. If it's not, you can deploy your own Clipper Service contract.

327
00:58:18.190 --> 00:58:29.830
Richard Bair: Again, it's decentralized. If you look at the Clipper service and you like it, you could look at the verifier contract that's being used on the channel and decide if you trust that verifier contract.

328
00:58:29.830 --> 00:58:46.140
Richard Bair: If you trust the verifier contract, you can use the channel. You can send messages between two ledgers. And if you don't, you can deploy your own verifier contract and set up your own channel and be able to communicate between ledgers. So again, it's like, it's… that was really central and core to our design principles.

329
00:58:46.300 --> 00:58:59.440
Richard Bair: Clipper is intended as a gift to the world, a technical solution to cross-ledger communication that does not centralize any aspect of the system.

330
00:58:59.600 --> 00:59:17.120
Richard Bair: does not extract rents from anywhere in the system, and it's truly meant as the TCP IP of the blockchain world, a core protocol that enables communication between ledgers.

331
00:59:17.120 --> 00:59:25.090
Richard Bair: And, all the value is going to be built on top of Clipper, not extracted from people using Clipper. So…

332
00:59:26.020 --> 00:59:34.210
Richard Bair: That's, that, that's the, sort of, initial positioning and high-level view. Encourage…

